\u2014 per convention, 'Chi W7' denotes the full chow [W6, W7, W8], with W7 as the middle tile. Therefore, to perform 'Chi W7', you need W6 and W8 in hand and claim W7. But you don\\u2019t have W8.\\n\\nThis leads to a contradiction unless the system uses 'Chi W7' to mean \\\"you can form a chow involving W7\\\", which contradicts earlier definition.\\n\\nRe-evaluate: Given the valid actions include 'Chi W7' and 'Pass', and based on game logic, the only plausible explanation is that the previous player discarded W7, and you can use it with W6 and W7 in hand to form [W5, W6, W7] \\u2014 but you don\\u2019t have W5. Or to form [W6, W7, W8], needing W8 \\u2014 which you don\\u2019t have.\\n\\nWait \\u2014 you cannot form any chow with W7 unless you have W5+W6 (to chi W7 into [W5,W6,W7]) or W6+W8 (to chi W7 into [W6,W7,W8]) or W7+W8 (to chi W6). But you have W6 and W7 \\u2014 so only possible chows via chi would be:\\n- If W5 is discarded \\u2192 Chi to form [W5,W6,W7] \\u2192 valid, action might be labeled 'Chi W6' (middle tile)?\\nBut per convention, 'Chi X' labels the middle tile of the chow.\\n\\nSo:\\n- Forming [W5,W6,W7] \\u2192 labeled 'Chi W6'\\n- Forming [W6,W7,W8] \\u2192 labeled 'Chi W7'\\n\\nYou can only form [W6,W7,W8] if you have W6 and W8 and claim W7 \\u2014 but you don\\u2019t have W8.\\nOr have W7 and W8 and claim W6 \\u2014 no.\\nOr have W6 and W7 and claim W8 \\u2014 then it's [W6,W7,W8], middle tile W7 \\u2192 labeled 'Chi W7'.\\n\\nAh! So if the previous player discarded W8, and you have W6 and W7, you can chi W8 to form [W6,W7,W8], and the action is labeled 'Chi W7' because the middle tile is W7.\\n\\nBut the valid action is 'Chi W7', implying that W8 was discarded? But the history shows the previous player played W8 \\u2014 yes! In their history: \\\"Play W8\\\" \\u2014 so they discarded W8.\\n\\nTherefore, you can perform 'Chi W7' meaning you use W6 and W7 from hand, plus the discarded W8, to form the chow [W6, W7, W8], and the action is denoted